The main limit is **the serve-hold structure itself**: in men's Grand Slam tennis the server wins roughly 80% of service games, so a returning player who improves still has to convert a small number of break-point chances against a server who is, on average, favored in every point.

Concretely, the trailing player's improved returning raises pressure but does not change the fact that:
- they only get break chances on a minority of points, and those chances are often saved by a good first serve;
- a single lost break point resets the game, and the opponent then serves out the next game comfortably;
- the trailing player must also hold their own serve throughout, so any lapse on their own service game cancels the gain.

So the pressure converts into games only if the improved returning is sustained across multiple return games and paired with holding serve — otherwise it produces points and deuce games, not breaks. This is an empirical regularity, not a precise figure.

The main thing that breaks a server's first serve under break-point pressure is **loss of rhythm and margin on the first delivery itself** — the server starts steering the ball rather than swinging freely, so first-serve percentage drops and second serves become the norm.

Concretely, the pressure causes the server to:
- aim closer to lines or hit a safer, slower first serve, both of which reduce the percentage in;
- rush the motion (tighter arm, earlier contact), producing more faults;
- then face the returner from a second serve, where the returner is far more aggressive.

So the first serve breaks first because it is the shot most sensitive to tension and the one the server consciously tries to control. Once the first serve is gone, the whole game structure collapses toward the returner. This is an empirical regularity, not a precise figure.

Late in the set — specifically **after the 4th–5th game and especially from around 4–4 onward**, and most acutely in the **closing games of the set** (games 8–12, including tiebreaks).

The pattern: players are freshest at the start of a set, and the physical and mental cost accumulates across the set. The heaviest fatigue shows in:
- long deuce games and long rallies late in the set;
- the game immediately after a long game (the "letdown" game);
- tiebreaks, where concentration and leg drive are most taxed.

Across a five-set match, this compounds — the deepest fatigue appears in the **4th and 5th sets**, and within those sets the late-set games are where running out is most visible. Note this is an empirical judgment about a general tendency, not a measured figure; individual fitness, match duration, and rally length shift it considerably.

